    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `NameChecker.leave_module`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `leave_module` function is a method within the `NameChecker` class, designed to analyze a Python module after it has been fully parsed. This function's primary purpose is to identify and report naming issues within the module, specifically focusing on groups of names that are considered \"bad\" or problematic. It takes a single parameter, `_`, which is an instance of `nodes.Module`, representing the module being analyzed. Although the parameter is named `_`, it is not used within the function, indicating that it is a placeholder for a parameter that is not needed for the function's operation.\n\nThe function iterates over the `_bad_names` dictionary, which contains groups of names categorized by some criteria (not specified in the provided code snippet). For each group, if the group contains fewer than two items, the function skips further processing for that group. If a group has two or more items, the function proceeds to organize these groups by their size, using a `defaultdict` to collect groups of the same size. It also determines the smallest group size, `min_warnings`, and identifies the most prevalent group based on its size.\n\nThe function then sorts the groups of the smallest size by the line number of the first warning in each group. If there is more than one group of the smallest size, it selects all but the first group as warnings to be reported. If there is only one group of the smallest size, it selects all items in that group as warnings. Finally, the function iterates over the selected warnings and raises a name warning using the `_raise_name_warning` method, passing the prevalent group and the warning details.\n\nThe function does not return any value; its primary output is the raising of warnings through the `_raise_name_warning` method. It relies on the `_bad_names` dictionary and the `_raise_name_warning` method for its operation. The function handles edge cases by skipping groups with fewer than two items and by selecting the most prevalent group for reporting purposes. It does not explicitly handle exceptions or errors, as the code snippet does not include any exception handling mechanisms. The side effects of this function include the potential raising of warnings, which could affect the overall linting process or the developer's understanding of the code quality.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
